{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-50351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the slope of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta $, at $\\theta =\\pi $,  given that \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\frac{1}{\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-50351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the rule for differentiating parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the numerator and denominator at $\\theta =\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\pi ) & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot (-1)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi } & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\pi )}{{x}^{′}(\\pi )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi }{(-\\frac{1}{\\pi })} \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-50351" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d7dba1b9dfbf7e71738309d2e85f2d4dfb2c096ee54f5c41eea266398abf399"} {"question" "q-50351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-50351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi }"] ["math" "-\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi "] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi "] ["math" "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-50351" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-348107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/assets/70c737e1fbbf63b3551763e48f4d7a956ffcc04ff68cced4f813b28e87630eb3.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-348107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }{({e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[({e}^{2\\theta })^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\pi }[2{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +{e}^{2\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\frac{{e}^{2\\theta }}{2\\pi }(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-348107" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d7dba1b9dfbf7e71738309d2e85f2d4dfb2c096ee54f5c41eea266398abf399"} {"question" "q-348107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-348107" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sin(\\theta)+\\cos(\\theta)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the slope $m$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$m=$ \n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nWe have,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8}) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, the slope $m$ of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /8} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})}{{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353217" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d7dba1b9dfbf7e71738309d2e85f2d4dfb2c096ee54f5c41eea266398abf399"} {"question" "q-353217" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353217" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the polar curve $r=22,$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=22,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =-22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{22\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{-22\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353229" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8d7dba1b9dfbf7e71738309d2e85f2d4dfb2c096ee54f5c41eea266398abf399"} {"question" "q-353229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353229" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\cot(\\theta)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/assets/7b11eccd4d3e5e1e90565219ce9b5098cd5aaeafe67c64d5e148245e17a6d355.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi },$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ | $=$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi },$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{((2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta $ using the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }{((2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )]}{\\frac{1}{3\\pi }[\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ]} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068032/state.json" "file_sha256" "14493a061dfff12d5a968605b3ab7811809bcbcf35fc900c5521e4753882fe57"} {"question" "q-353245" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "